  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1203 PM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nSHOWERS AND THUNDERSTORMS TODAY: \n\nToday looks to be our final day of widespread showers and\nthunderstorms from the current push of monsoonal moisture. \nToday's sounding shows a decrease in both precipitable water \n(PW) and instability. However, the PW value of 1.04 inches \nexceeds the 90th percentile for August 31st at GJT, which shows  \nthat atmospheric moisture is still abundant. The reduction in \ninstability and persisting widespread cloud cover could act to \ndampen today's convection, mostly during the early afternoon.  \nHowever, a shortwave trough, in addition to a bit of jet support\nand difluence aloft, will support more storms this evening even\nduring the absence of surface heating. Many storms will \nefficiently produce rain, which could lead to localized areas of\nflash flooding from heavy rainfall. Primary areas of concern \nfor flooding are recent burn scars, slot canyons, slick rock \nareas, creeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone \nlocations. Most of eastern Utah and western Colorado remains in\na Flood Watch through midnight tonight. \n\nDRIER TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY:\n\nAtmospheric moisture will decrease tomorrow, leading to\ndecreased precipitation chances. Afternoon convection will still\nbe possible, particularly on Tuesday and Friday, but chances\nfavor the higher terrain. High temperatures will gradually\nincrease through the workweek, returning to near normal by\nFriday. \n\nMOISTURE RETURNS THIS WEEKEND: \n\nGuidance suggests a strong push of tropical moisture late in \nthe workweek and into the weekend. The ECMWF Ens and GFS Ens  \nare presently in relatively good agreement regarding the timing\nand magnitude of this moisture. However, changes with the \ntropical system activity in the Pacific could substantially \nalter this forecast, so be sure to stay tuned for updates on \nhow this weekend's forecast develops.",
